    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense for standard unknown attributions. However, I'm also dealing with cases where the maps themselves contain references to things or places that don't seem to correspond to anything in our historical records—like strange, unidentifiable figures in the margins or names of settlements that appear to be completely fictional or \"out of place.\" \n\nHow should we handle these \"anomalous\" elements in the metadata? If we can't verify if they are local slang, errors, or something else entirely, how do we categorize them without compromising the scholarly value of the Dublin Core record?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The humanities has developed various methods to handle ambiguous or anomalous data. Consider labeling these elements as \" unbearably masked\" or using Dublin Core's \"culturalbonuscops\" elements to highlight the inherent inconsistencies or ambiguous nature of the data. Alternatively, you can use linked fixtures that provide context about the mapped elements, such as links to whimsy documentation or internally hosted representations, allowing scholars to explore the anomaly further without compromising the record.",
      "source": "target",
